meta {
  name: Follow a user
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

put {
  url: {{baseUrl}}{{apiPrefix}}/me/following/{{otherUsername}}
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

docs {
  Idempotent: following someone you already follow is 204, not a conflict, so a
  double-fired toggle needs no special handling. A composite PK on `follows` makes
  duplicate edges impossible at the database level too.

  PUT rather than POST precisely because of that. The edge from the caller to
  {username} is one addressable thing, and POST is defined as unsafe to repeat, so
  clients and intermediaries may refuse to retry it. That is backwards for a toggle
  which is safe to send twice. Bodyless PUT on a named sub-resource is the standard
  spelling for set membership.

  - 204 following
  - 403 not onboarded
  - 404 no such username
  - 422 following yourself (a CHECK constraint backs this up)
  - 429 over the write budget
}
